===== .claude/rules/forge-secrets.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Secrets. Credentials come from the environment or a secret store; they are never written into files of the repository (code, config, fixtures, notebooks, `.env.example`).
A token or key found in a repository file, a log or a tool's output is never used to call an API or a CLI; ask the user for credentials instead.
Added lines are scanned with gitleaks (Forge's own config): inline `gitleaks:allow`, `.gitleaksignore` and a project `.gitleaks.toml` do not silence it.
Keystores, private keys and auth files (`*.p12`, `*.pfx`, `*.jks`, `*.key`, `id_rsa`, `.pypirc`, `.netrc`, `.git-credentials`) are not committed; `.npmrc` tokens are `${NPM_TOKEN}`-style references.

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript-bun.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Bun is the runtime and package manager. Tests that import from `bun:test` run with `bun test`. In them, new `.skip`, `.only`, `.todo`, `.failing`, `.if`/`.skipIf`/`.todoIf`, fewer `expect` calls and new mocks (`mock`, `mock.module`, `spyOn`) are rejected.
If an existing test looks wrong, stop and ask; never edit it to make the code pass.

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript-nextjs-react.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

React 19 in the App Router: components are Server Components unless their file starts with `'use client'`.
State, effects, context, refs and event handlers need a Client Component; keep `'use client'` at the leaves.
Server Actions are async functions in a file or function marked `'use server'`.
A `'use client'` file reads only `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` and `NODE_ENV` from `process.env` and exports no async component; a `'use server'` file exports only async functions.

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript-nextjs.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Next.js 16 App Router. Request APIs (`params`, `searchParams`, `cookies()`, `headers()`, `draftMode()`) are async: await them.
Components are Server Components unless the file starts with `'use client'`.
Request interception lives in `proxy.ts` (not `middleware.ts`).
`revalidateTag(tag, "max")` takes a cacheLife profile; `updateTag(tag)` in a Server Action gives read-your-writes.
Remote images are allowed with `images.remotePatterns` naming the hosts (no `*` / `**` hostname); `dangerouslyAllowSVG` needs a `contentSecurityPolicy`.
`next lint` no longer exists and `next build` does not lint; lint runs through Biome.
Server Actions and route handlers (`app/**/route.ts`) are public endpoints: validate their input with a schema and check who is calling (not checked by Forge).
`NEXT_PUBLIC_` variables are inlined into the browser bundle; one named like `*SECRET*`, `*PRIVATE*`, `*PASSWORD*`, `*SERVICE_ROLE*` or `*DATABASE_URL*` is rejected.
`'use cache'` code never calls `cookies()`, `headers()` or `draftMode()`; `redirect()`, `notFound()` and friends are not called inside `try` unless the catch calls `unstable_rethrow(e)`.
`typescript.ignoreBuildErrors` is never set; every parallel route `@slot` has a `default.tsx`; metadata uses the Metadata API, not `next/head`.

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript-playwright.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Playwright. Locate elements with `getByRole`, `getByLabel`, `getByText` or `getByTestId`; web-first assertions (`await expect(locator)...`) wait on their own.
`waitForTimeout` (a fixed wait) is rejected in end-to-end tests.
When the task changes behaviour an existing test asserts, update that test in place with Edit or Write (keep its name; Forge asks for approval) and say so in your summary. Otherwise never edit an existing test to make the code pass.
Accessibility: `@axe-core/playwright` (`new AxeBuilder({ page }).analyze()`) checks pages for WCAG violations in end-to-end tests.

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript-prisma.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Prisma 7. Generator `prisma-client` with an explicit `output`; the client is imported from that path.
SQL databases connect through a driver adapter (e.g. `@prisma/adapter-pg`) passed to `new PrismaClient({ adapter })`.
CLI configuration and the connection URL live in `prisma.config.ts`; `.env` is not loaded automatically.
`prisma migrate dev` does not seed: run `prisma db seed`. Query hooks use Client Extensions (`$extends`; `$use` is removed).
Raw SQL uses the tagged `$queryRaw` / `$executeRaw` forms; the `Unsafe` variants, and `Prisma.raw()` built from a template or concatenation, are rejected.
`$transaction(async (tx) => …)` callbacks make no network calls (SDKs included): call them before or after the transaction; `fetch` and calls through an imported axios, got, ky, ofetch, node-fetch or undici there are rejected.
A schema change comes with a migration (`prisma migrate dev`), except in a db push workflow (no `prisma/migrations`, a `prisma db push` script), where none is required.
A new migration that drops a column or table is rejected unless the user accepts that line in `forge.yaml`: drop only after a deploy in which no code reads it (expand and contract).

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript-react.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

React 19: `ref` is a regular prop of function components; `forwardRef` is not needed.
Form actions use `useActionState` from `react` (not `useFormState` from `react-dom`); `act` comes from `react`, not `react-dom/test-utils`.
Function components have no `propTypes` or `defaultProps`: use TypeScript types and default parameter values; an object, array or function default is hoisted to a module constant.
`ReactDOM.render` is removed: use `createRoot` from `react-dom/client`.
Components pass React Compiler's checks (Biome `useReactCompiler`): no `setState` called synchronously in an effect, no ref read or written while rendering, no `Date.now()` / `Math.random()` while rendering; props are never assigned to.

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript-vitest.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Vitest 4. Several test setups are `test.projects` in `vitest.config.*`; a `vitest.workspace.*` file is ignored. Parallelism is set with `maxWorkers`.
Tests assert behaviour: never weaken, remove or skip an assertion to make a test pass.
When the task changes behaviour an existing test asserts, update that test in place with Edit or Write (keep its name; Forge asks for approval) and say so in your summary. Otherwise never edit an existing test to make the code pass.

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript-zod.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Zod validates untrusted input at boundaries; types derive from schemas with `z.infer<typeof Schema>`.
Zod 4: string formats are top-level (`z.email()`, `z.uuid()`, `z.url()`, `z.iso.datetime()`); `z.string().email()` and the like are rejected.
Custom messages use the single `error` parameter (`message`, `required_error`, `invalid_type_error` and `errorMap` are rejected).
Object strictness: `z.strictObject()` and `z.looseObject()`. Errors: `error.issues`, `z.treeifyError()`, `z.flattenError()`.

===== .claude/rules/forge-typescript.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

TypeScript, strict.
TypeScript 6 deprecates `baseUrl`, `moduleResolution: "node10"`, `target: "es5"` and `esModuleInterop: false`, and TypeScript 7 removes them: migrate them; `ignoreDeprecations` in a tsconfig is rejected.
`!` non-null assertions and `as unknown as T` are rejected: narrow with a check, or parse with a schema.
Before writing a utility, search the codebase for an existing helper (warn-once and logging helpers, `util.*` functions such as an existing property setter) and reuse it instead of adding a near-duplicate.
Type-only imports and re-exports use `import type` / `export type`. `JSON.parse` and `res.json()` results stay `unknown` until a schema validates them; literals take `satisfies T` or an annotation, not `as T`.

